<node id="Geopolymer Research Study">
  <data key="d0">Geopolymer Research Study</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">The Geopolymer Research Study refers to the scientific investigation into improving the mechanical properties of fly ash-based geopolymer by adding mullite, conducted by researchers from Mae Fah Luang University and the National Metal and Materials Technology Centre.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-69a2fbf09309e0a17bac00036bbe5fc0</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_9780470456200.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754040002</data>
</node>
<node id="Fly Ash Geopolymer-Mullite Composites">
  <data key="d0">Fly Ash Geopolymer-Mullite Composites</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Fly Ash Geopolymer-Mullite Composites are a class of construction materials made by reinforcing geopolymer derived from class C fly ash with mullite powder to enhance compressive strength.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-69a2fbf09309e0a17bac00036bbe5fc0</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_9780470456200.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754040004</data>
</node>
<node id="Compressive Strength Enhancement">
  <data key="d0">Compressive Strength Enhancement</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Compressive Strength Enhancement refers to the improvement in mechanical performance of geopolymer materials achieved through the addition of mullite, as demonstrated in the study.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-69a2fbf09309e0a17bac00036bbe5fc0</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_9780470456200.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754040004</data>
</node>
<node id="Class C Fly Ash">
  <data key="d0">Class C Fly Ash</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Class C fly ash is a type of fly ash collected from the Mae Moh power plant and used as a base material for geopolymer production in the study.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-69a2fbf09309e0a17bac00036bbe5fc0</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_9780470456200.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754040005</data>
</node>
<node id="Mullite Powder">
  <data key="d0">Mullite Powder</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Mullite powder is a material added to geopolymer mixtures to form composites, with varying weight percentages used in the study.&lt;SEP&gt;Mullite powder is an industrial-grade crystalline aluminosilicate material added to fly ash geopolymer to improve mechanical properties, sourced from a local supplier in Thailand.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-69a2fbf09309e0a17bac00036bbe5fc0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2406de4f3a87655b6e75f02341089818</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_9780470456200.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754040007</data>
</node>
<node id="Alkaline Activator">
  <data key="d0">Alkaline Activator</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Alkaline activator is a mixture of chemical-grade sodium hydroxide solution and commercial sodium silicate used to activate the fly ash in geopolymer synthesis.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-69a2fbf09309e0a17bac00036bbe5fc0</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_9780470456200.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754040008</data>
</node>
<node id="Physical Properties">
  <data key="d0">Physical Properties</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Physical properties include porosity, density, and water absorption of the geopolymer-mullite composites, determined following ASTM C-642.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-69a2fbf09309e0a17bac00036bbe5fc0</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_9780470456200.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754040010</data>
</node>
<node id="Microstructure">
  <data key="d0">Microstructure</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Microstructure refers to the internal structure of geopolymer composites, including mullite crystal distribution and interlocking, which influences mechanical performance.&lt;SEP&gt;Microstructure refers to the internal structure of the composites analyzed in relation to their mechanical and physical properties, though specific details are not described in the text.&lt;SEP&gt;Microstructure refers to the internal structure of the geopolymer, analyzed using SEM and influenced by NaOH concentration as shown in Figure 7.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-69a2fbf09309e0a17bac00036bbe5fc0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2406de4f3a87655b6e75f02341089818&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0ff7939cdf3c7690a9b0936aae778017</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_9780470456200.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754040011</data>
</node>
<node id="ASTM C-642">
  <data key="d0">ASTM C-642</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">ASTM C-642 is a standard method followed for determining porosity, density, and water absorption of geopolymer samples.&lt;SEP&gt;ASTM C-642 is the standard test method used to determine porosity, density, and water absorption of the composite samples in the study.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-69a2fbf09309e0a17bac00036bbe5fc0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2406de4f3a87655b6e75f02341089818</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_9780470456200.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754040012</data>
</node>
<node id="Sodium Hydroxide Solution">
  <data key="d0">Sodium Hydroxide Solution</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Sodium hydroxide solution is a chemical-grade reagent used as part of the alkaline activator in the geopolymer synthesis process.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-69a2fbf09309e0a17bac00036bbe5fc0</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_9780470456200.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754040013</data>
</node>
<node id="Sodium Silicate">
  <data key="d0">Sodium Silicate</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Sodium silicate (Na₂SiO₃) is a liquid component used in the geopolymer mixture, combined with NaOH to form the alkaline activator solution.&lt;SEP&gt;Sodium silicate is a commercial chemical used in combination with sodium hydroxide as an alkaline activator in geopolymer preparation.&lt;SEP&gt;Sodium silicate is a commercial product used in combination with sodium hydroxide as the alkaline activator for geopolymer formation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-69a2fbf09309e0a17bac00036bbe5fc0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2406de4f3a87655b6e75f02341089818&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ab8d7ecaf50775e77f92ac39831ef997</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_9780470456200.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754040013</data>
</node>
<node id="Laser Diffraction Analysis">
  <data key="d0">Laser Diffraction Analysis</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Laser diffraction analysis is the method used to measure particle size distribution of fly ash and mullite powder, performed using a Malvern Instrument Mastersizer 2000.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-69a2fbf09309e0a17bac00036bbe5fc0</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_9780470456200.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754040015</data>
</node>
<node id="X-ray Fluorescence">
  <data key="d0">X-ray Fluorescence</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">X-ray Fluorescence is the analytical technique used to determine the chemical composition of fly ash and mullite powder in the study.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-69a2fbf09309e0a17bac00036bbe5fc0</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_9780470456200.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754040015</data>
</node>
<node id="X-ray Diffraction">
  <data key="d0">X-ray Diffraction</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">X-ray Diffraction is the method used to analyze the crystalline phases of raw materials, as shown in Figure 2 of the study.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-69a2fbf09309e0a17bac00036bbe5fc0</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_9780470456200.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754040017</data>
</node>
<node id="SEM">
  <data key="d0">SEM</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">SEM (Scanning Electron Microscopy) is used to capture images of the raw materials, showing the spherical vitreous particles of fly ash and irregular shape of mullite powder.&lt;SEP&gt;SEM refers to scanning electron microscopy used to analyze the microstructure of geopolymers and observe unreacted fly ash particles.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-69a2fbf09309e0a17bac00036bbe5fc0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db7821ac60e19e40b14255f9d1d086d1</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_9780470456200.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754040018</data>
</node>
<node id="Malvern Instrument Mastersizer 2000">
  <data key="d0">Malvern Instrument Mastersizer 2000</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">Malvern Instrument is a company that manufactured the Mastersizer 2000, a device used for laser diffraction analysis to measure particle distribution.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2406de4f3a87655b6e75f02341089818</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_9780470456200.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754040020</data>
</node>
<node id="Sodium Hydroxide">
  <data key="d0">Sodium Hydroxide</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Sodium hydroxide is a chemical used in the preparation of the alkaline activator for geopolymer synthesis.&lt;SEP&gt;Sodium hydroxide is an analytical-grade chemical used in the geopolymerization process to react with fly ash for synthesizing geopolymers.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ad60afc1fc1390582346359c09ae89a7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2406de4f3a87655b6e75f02341089818</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_9780470456200.md&lt;SEP&gt;doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208113</data>
</node>
<node id="Fly Ash">
  <data key="d0">Fly Ash</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Fly Ash (FA) is a by-product generated from coal combustion in electric power plants, primarily composed of 40–50 wt% silica (silicon dioxide), along with significant amounts of alumina (aluminum oxide), iron oxide, calcium oxide, magnesium oxide, sulfur oxide, potassium oxide, sodium, titanium dioxide, strontium oxide, phosphorus pentoxide, and residual carbon. It is characterized by spherical particles, including hollow cenospheres, with a particle size distribution ranging from less than 1 μm to over 50 μm, though the typical size is less than 20 μm, with approximately 10% of particles exceeding 30 μm. Its chemical composition and physical morphology make it suitable for various industrial applications.

Fly ash serves as a primary aluminosilicate source in the production of geopolymers. Due to its glassy structure, it can be dissolved under alkaline conditions when mixed with alkaline activators, enabling geopolymerization. In this context, it acts as a raw material in geopolymer mixtures, where its particle size distribution and chemical profile are critical for performance. It is widely used as a pozzolan in geopolymer synthesis and is valued for its reactivity in alkaline environments.

In addition to its role in geopolymer composites, fly ash is also utilized as a filler material in natural rubber (NR) composites. However, when used in rubber applications, it exhibits poorer dispersion within the matrix, leading to larger agglomerates and increased void content compared to geopolymer-based fillers. This results in weaker interfacial interactions with the rubber matrix and reduced thermal stability. Despite these limitations, fly ash is explored as a low-cost, natural filler alternative in rubber compounds, with ongoing research focusing on its characterization in terms of particle size, chemical composition, morphology, and effects on curing behavior.

In summary, fly ash is a versatile industrial byproduct with dual applications: as a key raw material in geopolymer production due to its reactive aluminosilicate content, and as a filler in natural rubber composites, where its performance is limited by dispersion challenges and inferior matrix compatibility compared to geopolymer fillers.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b59a215607da3067fe396c2706185342&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0ff7939cdf3c7690a9b0936aae778017&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ab8d7ecaf50775e77f92ac39831ef997&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2b41c1604bb36ddf18d8b7e29a9c6dbe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ee381638b4e2088dd8f91df9d74e6da7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2406de4f3a87655b6e75f02341089818&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-93bfd54d23eafa9bcd4bd9cf8880c1cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5032c26dab6030ab94a51cdae3644229&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be33bdf2df99a882230f2e5cf5a15dcf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ad60afc1fc1390582346359c09ae89a7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5e2d2a2aac672793717ce07dc518653a</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_9780470456200.md&lt;SEP&gt;doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208112</data>
</node>
<node id="Compressive Strength">
  <data key="d0">Compressive Strength</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Compressive strength is a mechanical property of geopolymer composites, measured in megapascals (MPa), that indicates the material's resistance to failure under compressive load. It was evaluated after 7 days of curing using a Universal Testing Machine, with each reported value representing the average of five samples. The addition of mullite, up to 40 wt%, significantly enhances compressive strength, as demonstrated by an increase from 14 MPa to 71 MPa in the geopolymer composite. This improvement is attributed not only to mullite incorporation but also potentially to reduced water content, although specific values related to the latter factor are not provided in the data. Overall, compressive strength is a key performance indicator influenced by both compositional and processing factors in geopolymer systems.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-69a2fbf09309e0a17bac00036bbe5fc0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ab8d7ecaf50775e77f92ac39831ef997&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2406de4f3a87655b6e75f02341089818&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0ff7939cdf3c7690a9b0936aae778017&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db7821ac60e19e40b14255f9d1d086d1</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_9780470456200.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754040022</data>
</node>
<node id="Geopolymer-Mullite Composite">
  <data key="d0">Geopolymer-Mullite Composite</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Geopolymer-mullite composite is a material formed by adding mullite powder to a geopolymer mixture, studied for its mechanical and structural properties.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2406de4f3a87655b6e75f02341089818</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_9780470456200.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754040023</data>
</node>
<node id="ASTM C-191">
  <data key="d0">ASTM C-191</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">ASTM C-191 is a standard procedure used for testing the setting time of geopolymer paste.&lt;SEP&gt;ASTM C-191 is the standard method used to measure the setting time of geopolymer mixtures at 10-minute intervals.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2406de4f3a87655b6e75f02341089818&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0ff7939cdf3c7690a9b0936aae778017</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_9780470456200.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754040024</data>
</node>
<node id="Vicat Apparatus">
  <data key="d0">Vicat Apparatus</data>
  <data key="d1">equipment</data>
  <data key="d2">The Vicat apparatus is a manually operated device used to test the setting time of geopolymer paste according to ASTM C-191.&lt;SEP&gt;Vicat apparatus is used to measure the initial and final setting time of geopolymer mixtures at 10-minute intervals in accordance with ASTM C-191.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2406de4f3a87655b6e75f02341089818&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0ff7939cdf3c7690a9b0936aae778017</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_9780470456200.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754040026</data>
</node>
<node id="Universal Testing Machine (Instron 5566)">
  <data key="d0">Universal Testing Machine (Instron 5566)</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">Instron 5566 is a Universal Testing Machine used to measure the compressive strength of samples at a specified loading speed.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2406de4f3a87655b6e75f02341089818</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_9780470456200.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754040027</data>
</node>
<node id="Scanning Electron Microscopy (SEM)">
  <data key="d0">Scanning Electron Microscopy (SEM)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Scanning Electron Microscopy is a technique used to analyze the morphology of polished sample surfaces, with samples prepared using specific grinding and polishing procedures.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2406de4f3a87655b6e75f02341089818</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_9780470456200.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754040029</data>
</node>
<node id="SiC Grinding Paper">
  <data key="d0">SiC Grinding Paper</data>
  <data key="d1">equipment</data>
  <data key="d2">SiC grinding paper, in grades P600 and P1200, is used for polishing samples prior to SEM observation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2406de4f3a87655b6e75f02341089818</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_9780470456200.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754040029</data>
</node>
<node id="Diamond Particles">
  <data key="d0">Diamond Particles</data>
  <data key="d1">equipment</data>
  <data key="d2">6μm diamond particles are used in the final stage of sample polishing before SEM analysis.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2406de4f3a87655b6e75f02341089818</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_9780470456200.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754040031</data>
</node>
<node id="Gold Coating">
  <data key="d0">Gold Coating</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">Gold coating is a process applied via sputtering under vacuum to prepare rubber specimens for imaging using scanning electron microscopy.&lt;SEP&gt;Gold coating is applied to samples prior to SEM observation to enhance conductivity and image quality.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ee381638b4e2088dd8f91df9d74e6da7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2406de4f3a87655b6e75f02341089818</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_9780470456200.md&lt;SEP&gt;doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208219</data>
</node>
<node id="Al Bakri et al., 2012">
  <data key="d0">Al Bakri et al., 2012</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Al Bakri et al., 2012 refers to a research group or individual cited for prior work on geopolymer strength in relation to NaOH concentration.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2406de4f3a87655b6e75f02341089818</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_9780470456200.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754040033</data>
</node>
<node id="Arioz et al., 2012">
  <data key="d0">Arioz et al., 2012</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Arioz et al., 2012 refers to a research group or individual cited alongside Al Bakri et al. for supporting previous findings on NaOH concentration effects.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2406de4f3a87655b6e75f02341089818</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_9780470456200.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754040033</data>
</node>
<node id="Guo et al., 2010">
  <data key="d0">Guo et al., 2010</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Guo et al., 2010 is referenced as a source discussing the dissolution of fly ash's glassy structure in geopolymer synthesis.&lt;SEP&gt;Guo et al., 2010 refers to a research group or individual cited for explaining the role of strong alkalis in dissolving silicon and aluminum in fly ash.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2406de4f3a87655b6e75f02341089818&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0ff7939cdf3c7690a9b0936aae778017</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_9780470456200.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754040035</data>
</node>
<node id="TABLE 2">
  <data key="d0">TABLE 2</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">TABLE 2 presents the particle distribution data of fly ash and mullite powder as measured by laser diffraction analysis.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2406de4f3a87655b6e75f02341089818</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_9780470456200.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754040036</data>
</node>
<node id="Figure 1">
  <data key="d0">Figure 1</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Figure 1 illustrates the geopolymerization process based on the reaction between fly ash and alkaline solution.&lt;SEP&gt;Figure 1 shows SEM pictures of raw materials used in the geopolymer composite study.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2406de4f3a87655b6e75f02341089818&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5e2d2a2aac672793717ce07dc518653a</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_9780470456200.md&lt;SEP&gt;doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208259</data>
</node>
<node id="Figure 2">
  <data key="d0">Figure 2</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Figure 2 displays the X-ray diffractogram of the raw materials used in the study.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2406de4f3a87655b6e75f02341089818</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_9780470456200.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754040038</data>
</node>
<node id="Figure 3">
  <data key="d0">Figure 3</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Figure 3 illustrates the effect of sodium hydroxide concentration on the compressive strength of geopolymers.&lt;SEP&gt;Figure 3 presents SEM images used to assess the particle morphologies of geopolymer and fly ash, showing differences in surface texture and shape between the two materials.&lt;SEP&gt;Figure 3 presents scanning electron microscopy micrographs of geopolymer and fly ash particles.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2406de4f3a87655b6e75f02341089818&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5e2d2a2aac672793717ce07dc518653a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2b41c1604bb36ddf18d8b7e29a9c6dbe</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_9780470456200.md&lt;SEP&gt;doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208224</data>
</node>
<node id="Figure 7">
  <data key="d0">Figure 7</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Figure 7 illustrates microstructural features of composites, highlighting voids, cavities, and filler aggregates that contribute to failure in over-filled rubber materials.&lt;SEP&gt;Figure 7 presents scanning electron microscopy micrographs of various natural rubber composites at 1,000× magnification, illustrating surface morphology and filler dispersion.&lt;SEP&gt;Figure 7 presents the effect of NaOH concentration on the microstructures of the geopolymer material.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c7f44c7e75058cc491eece367b71f639&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b59a215607da3067fe396c2706185342&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2406de4f3a87655b6e75f02341089818</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_9780470456200.md&lt;SEP&gt;doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208292</data>
</node>
<node id="PVC Molds">
  <data key="d0">PVC Molds</data>
  <data key="d1">equipment</data>
  <data key="d2">Cylindrical PVC molds of dimensions ∅15 mm × 30 mm were used to cast the geopolymer samples during preparation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2406de4f3a87655b6e75f02341089818</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_9780470456200.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754040040</data>
</node>
<node id="Curing Procedure">
  <data key="d0">Curing Procedure</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The curing procedure involved heating samples at 90°C for 1 day followed by 40°C for 6 days to solidify the geopolymer-mullite composite.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2406de4f3a87655b6e75f02341089818</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_9780470456200.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754040041</data>
</node>
<node id="Setting Time">
  <data key="d0">Setting Time</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Setting time refers to the duration for geopolymer mixtures to begin and finish hardening, measured using Vicat apparatus and influenced by mullite content.&lt;SEP&gt;Setting time refers to the time taken for geopolymer paste to set, tested using a Vicat apparatus according to ASTM C-191.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2406de4f3a87655b6e75f02341089818&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0ff7939cdf3c7690a9b0936aae778017</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_9780470456200.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754040042</data>
</node>
<node id="Porosity">
  <data key="d0">Porosity</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Porosity of the geopolymer was determined following ASTM C-642, along with density and water absorption measurements.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2406de4f3a87655b6e75f02341089818</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_9780470456200.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754040042</data>
</node>
<node id="Water Absorption">
  <data key="d0">Water Absorption</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Water absorption is a percentage indicating how much water a geopolymer can absorb, related to its porosity and void structure.&lt;SEP&gt;Water absorption of the geopolymer samples was measured according to ASTM C-642, with average values taken from five samples.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2406de4f3a87655b6e75f02341089818&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db7821ac60e19e40b14255f9d1d086d1</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_9780470456200.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754040043</data>
</node>
<node id="Optimum NaOH Concentration">
  <data key="d0">Optimum NaOH Concentration</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The optimum NaOH concentration was determined to be 12 mol/l based on consistent compressive strength results and microstructural analysis.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2406de4f3a87655b6e75f02341089818</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_9780470456200.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754040043</data>
</node>
<node id="Geopolymer">
  <data key="d0">Geopolymer</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Geopolymer (GP) is an aluminosilicate-based inorganic polymer synthesized through the geopolymerization of fly ash and an alkaline activator, forming an amorphous three-dimensional network. It is primarily derived from fly ash, exhibiting a particle size range from less than 1 μm to over 50 μm, with a typical size under 20 μm, and features smooth surfaces and irregular shapes similar to its precursor material. Chemical composition analysis reveals that geopolymer contains higher levels of silicon dioxide and lower calcium oxide compared to raw fly ash, while FTIR spectroscopy confirms the presence of Si-O-T bond vibrations, indicating successful geopolymerization. 

In the context of rubber composites, geopolymer is used as a novel filler material in natural rubber (NR) vulcanizates. It demonstrates improved dispersion within the rubber matrix, particularly at an optimal loading of 10 parts per hundred rubber (phr), leading to more homogeneous distribution. Its incorporation influences cure characteristics, including torque development, cross-linking density, and cure rate, while also contributing to slightly enhanced thermal stability compared to unfilled NR. Additionally, geopolymer-filled composites exhibit improved mechanical and dynamic properties, making it a promising sustainable filler option. 

Beyond rubber applications, geopolymer is recognized as a composite material capable of achieving high compressive strength and reduced cracking under optimal conditions. In some studies, it has been modified with additives such as mullite to tailor physical properties like setting time. Overall, geopolymer serves as a versatile material—both as a high-performance construction binder and as a functional filler in natural rubber composites—leveraging waste-derived aluminosilicate sources for advanced material applications.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b59a215607da3067fe396c2706185342&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0ff7939cdf3c7690a9b0936aae778017&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ab8d7ecaf50775e77f92ac39831ef997&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2b41c1604bb36ddf18d8b7e29a9c6dbe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ee381638b4e2088dd8f91df9d74e6da7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9f812eeaa3c93f5c4d945e0ae7c91d0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-93bfd54d23eafa9bcd4bd9cf8880c1cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5032c26dab6030ab94a51cdae3644229&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be33bdf2df99a882230f2e5cf5a15dcf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5e2d2a2aac672793717ce07dc518653a</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_9780470456200.md&lt;SEP&gt;doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208090</data>
</node>
<node id="Mullite">
  <data key="d0">Mullite</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Mullite is a ceramic material added to geopolymer composites to enhance compressive strength and modify microstructure, with optimal performance at 40 wt%.&lt;SEP&gt;Mullite is a ceramic material added to geopolymer mixtures, affecting setting time and water content. Its addition reduces initial setting time and proportionally decreases water content in the mixture.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ab8d7ecaf50775e77f92ac39831ef997&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0ff7939cdf3c7690a9b0936aae778017</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_9780470456200.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754040045</data>
</node>
<node id="NaOH Solution">
  <data key="d0">NaOH Solution</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">NaOH solution is an alkaline activator used in geopolymer synthesis, with concentration affecting fly ash dissolution and compressive strength.&lt;SEP&gt;NaOH solution is used as an alkaline activator in geopolymer preparation, with concentrations of 12 mol/l proving optimal for dissolving fly ash and reducing cracks.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-db7821ac60e19e40b14255f9d1d086d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0ff7939cdf3c7690a9b0936aae778017</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_9780470456200.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754040046</data>
</node>
<node id="Griffith's Principle">
  <data key="d0">Griffith's Principle</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Griffith's principle is cited to explain that specimens with larger pre-existing cracks exhibit lower compressive strength.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0ff7939cdf3c7690a9b0936aae778017</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_9780470456200.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754040048</data>
</node>
<node id="SEM Observation">
  <data key="d0">SEM Observation</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">SEM observation is used to detect unreacted fly ash particles and microstructural features in geopolymer-mullite composites.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0ff7939cdf3c7690a9b0936aae778017</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_9780470456200.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754040048</data>
</node>
<node id="Carty and Senapati, 1998">
  <data key="d0">Carty and Senapati, 1998</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Carty and Senapati, 1998 is cited for proposing that interlocking mullite crystals contribute to high strength in composite materials.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0ff7939cdf3c7690a9b0936aae778017</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_9780470456200.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754040049</data>
</node>
<node id="Figure 4">
  <data key="d0">Figure 4</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Figure 4 displays the FTIR spectra of geopolymer and fly ash, illustrating key absorption peaks related to Si-O-Si, Al-O-Al, O-H, and Si-O-T bonds, used to analyze geopolymerization reactions.&lt;SEP&gt;Figure 4 illustrates the effect of mullite addition on the compressive strength of geopolymers, showing strength values at different mullite weight percentages.&lt;SEP&gt;Figure 4 shows Fourier-transform infrared spectroscopy spectra of geopolymer and fly ash particles.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0ff7939cdf3c7690a9b0936aae778017&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5e2d2a2aac672793717ce07dc518653a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2b41c1604bb36ddf18d8b7e29a9c6dbe</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_9780470456200.md&lt;SEP&gt;doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208225</data>
</node>
<node id="Figure 5">
  <data key="d0">Figure 5</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Figure 5 displays XRD diffractograms that show the presence and increasing intensity of mullite peaks with higher mullite addition in geopolymer composites.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0ff7939cdf3c7690a9b0936aae778017</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_9780470456200.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754040052</data>
</node>
<node id="Figure 6">
  <data key="d0">Figure 6</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Figure 6 presents the effect of mullite addition on the setting time of geopolymers, indicating reduced initial and final setting times with increased mullite content.&lt;SEP&gt;Figure 6 provides visual evidence of filler dispersion in composites, showing agglomeration at higher geopolymer loadings, which correlates with reduced mechanical performance.&lt;SEP&gt;Figure 6 shows light microscopy images of gum natural rubber and filled composites, highlighting differences in surface roughness and filler dispersion.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c7f44c7e75058cc491eece367b71f639&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b59a215607da3067fe396c2706185342&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0ff7939cdf3c7690a9b0936aae778017</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_9780470456200.md&lt;SEP&gt;doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208293</data>
</node>
<node id="XRD Diffractograms">
  <data key="d0">XRD Diffractograms</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">XRD diffractograms are analytical data showing the crystalline phases in geopolymer composites, including mullite (Mu), faujasite (F), and quartz (Q), with peak intensity increasing with mullite content.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0ff7939cdf3c7690a9b0936aae778017</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_9780470456200.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754040053</data>
</node>
<node id="Needle-Like Structure">
  <data key="d0">Needle-Like Structure</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Needle-like structure refers to the morphology of mullite crystals observed at 40 wt% addition, contributing to interlocking and improved mechanical strength.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0ff7939cdf3c7690a9b0936aae778017</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_9780470456200.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754040055</data>
</node>
<node id="Solid to Liquid Ratio">
  <data key="d0">Solid to Liquid Ratio</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Solid to liquid ratio is a measured parameter in geopolymer preparation, varying from 1:0.60 to 1:0.37 depending on mullite addition, influencing workability and reactivity.&lt;SEP&gt;Solid to liquid ratio is a parameter affecting geopolymerization; a low ratio at 60 wt% mullite addition may lead to insufficient activator and unreacted fly ash particles.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ab8d7ecaf50775e77f92ac39831ef997&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0ff7939cdf3c7690a9b0936aae778017</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_9780470456200.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754040056</data>
</node>
<node id="Initial Setting Time">
  <data key="d0">Initial Setting Time</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Initial setting time is a physical property of geopolymer that measures the time taken for the paste to begin hardening. It decreases with increasing mullite addition up to 40 wt%, with no significant change beyond 60 wt%.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ab8d7ecaf50775e77f92ac39831ef997</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_9780470456200.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754040057</data>
</node>
<node id="Final Setting Time">
  <data key="d0">Final Setting Time</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Final setting time is the duration when the geopolymer paste fully hardens. At 20 wt% mullite addition, it was recorded as 3.2 hours.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ab8d7ecaf50775e77f92ac39831ef997</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_9780470456200.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754040058</data>
</node>
<node id="Water Content">
  <data key="d0">Water Content</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Water content in the geopolymer mixture is indirectly reduced by mullite addition, which may influence compressive strength and setting time.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ab8d7ecaf50775e77f92ac39831ef997</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_9780470456200.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754040059</data>
</node>
<node id="NaOH">
  <data key="d0">NaOH</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">NaOH is a liquid component used in the geopolymer mixture, specifically in a concentration of 8-16 mol/L, and is part of the alkaline activator solution.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ab8d7ecaf50775e77f92ac39831ef997</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_9780470456200.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754040060</data>
</node>
<node id="fly ash-based geopolymer">
  <data key="d0">fly ash-based geopolymer</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Fly ash-based geopolymer is a material with lower bulk and apparent density and higher volume of permeable voids, leading to higher water absorption.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-db7821ac60e19e40b14255f9d1d086d1</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_9780470456200.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754040060</data>
</node>
<node id="fly ash-mullite composite geopolymer">
  <data key="d0">fly ash-mullite composite geopolymer</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Fly ash-mullite composite geopolymer has higher density, lower volume of permeable voids, and lower water absorption due to the addition of mullite.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-db7821ac60e19e40b14255f9d1d086d1</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_9780470456200.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754040061</data>
</node>
<node id="alkaline activator concentration">
  <data key="d0">alkaline activator concentration</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Alkaline activator concentration affects the compressive strength of geopolymers, with 12 mol/l NaOH solution enabling effective dissolution of fly ash and higher strength.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-db7821ac60e19e40b14255f9d1d086d1</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_9780470456200.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754040062</data>
</node>
<node id="mullite">
  <data key="d0">mullite</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Mullite is added to fly ash-based geopolymer to increase compressive strength, reduce permeable voids, and lower water absorption.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-db7821ac60e19e40b14255f9d1d086d1</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_9780470456200.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754040064</data>
</node>
<node id="FA-Geopolymer">
  <data key="d0">FA-Geopolymer</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">FA-Geopolymer refers to a fly ash-based geopolymer with a bulk density of 1.65 g/cm³, apparent density of 2.21 g/cm³, 24.9% volume of permeable voids, and 12.86% water absorption.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-db7821ac60e19e40b14255f9d1d086d1</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_9780470456200.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754040066</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;FA-M 40 wt%">
  <data key="d0">"FA-M 40 wt%</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">FA-M 40 wt% refers to a fly ash-mullite composite geopolymer containing 40% mullite by weight, with improved properties including lower voids and water absorption.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-db7821ac60e19e40b14255f9d1d086d1</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_9780470456200.md</data>
