<node id="Natural Rubber Composites with Geopolymer Filler">
  <data key="d0">Natural Rubber Composites with Geopolymer Filler</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">The study titled 'Novel natural rubber composites with geopolymer filler' is a research activity investigating the properties of natural rubber composites using geopolymer particles as fillers.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5032c26dab6030ab94a51cdae3644229</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208076</data>
</node>
<node id="Cure Characteristics">
  <data key="d0">Cure Characteristics</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Cure characteristics describe the vulcanization behavior of rubber composites, which are influenced by the addition of fillers like geopolymers, fly ash, and RHA.&lt;SEP&gt;Cure characteristics refer to the changes in curing behavior of natural rubber composites when geopolymer particles are added, including faster curing due to metal oxide impurities.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5032c26dab6030ab94a51cdae3644229&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ad60afc1fc1390582346359c09ae89a7</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208080</data>
</node>
<node id="Mechanical Properties">
  <data key="d0">Mechanical Properties</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Mechanical properties include 100% modulus and tensile strength, which improved with the addition of 10 phr geopolymer in natural rubber composites.&lt;SEP&gt;Mechanical properties include tensile strength, Young's modulus, and elongation at break, which are evaluated in geopolymer or fly ash-filled natural rubber composites.&lt;SEP&gt;Mechanical properties of rubber composites are improved by geopolymer filler due to better dispersion and stronger filler-rubber interactions, as shown in Figure 9.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b59a215607da3067fe396c2706185342&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5032c26dab6030ab94a51cdae3644229&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ad60afc1fc1390582346359c09ae89a7</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208081</data>
</node>
<node id="Dynamic Properties">
  <data key="d0">Dynamic Properties</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Dynamic properties of the NR/geopolymer composites were studied, with the Payne effect observed in the filled composites.&lt;SEP&gt;Dynamic properties refer to the viscoelastic behavior of rubber composites under cyclic loading, investigated in geopolymer or fly ash-filled NR composites.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5032c26dab6030ab94a51cdae3644229&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ad60afc1fc1390582346359c09ae89a7</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208082</data>
</node>
<node id="Thermal Properties">
  <data key="d0">Thermal Properties</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Thermal properties describe how rubber composites respond to heat, including stability and degradation, and were studied in geopolymer or fly ash-filled NR composites.&lt;SEP&gt;Thermal properties were enhanced in the geopolymer-filled natural rubber composites, showing improved thermal stability.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5032c26dab6030ab94a51cdae3644229&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ad60afc1fc1390582346359c09ae89a7</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208082</data>
</node>
<node id="Morphological Properties">
  <data key="d0">Morphological Properties</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Morphological properties of the NR/geopolymer composites were investigated as part of the study.&lt;SEP&gt;Morphological properties refer to the microstructure and filler dispersion in rubber composites, analyzed in geopolymer or fly ash-filled NR composites.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5032c26dab6030ab94a51cdae3644229&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ad60afc1fc1390582346359c09ae89a7</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208084</data>
</node>
<node id="GP Particles">
  <data key="d0">GP Particles</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">GP particles are geopolymer particles used as fillers in natural rubber composites at various loadings (0, 10, 20, 30, 40 phr).</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5032c26dab6030ab94a51cdae3644229</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208084</data>
</node>
<node id="Filler Loading">
  <data key="d0">Filler Loading</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Filler loading refers to the amount of filler (e.g., GP or FA) added to the rubber matrix in phr (parts per hundred rubber); high GP content (30–40 phr) led to agglomeration and void formation.&lt;SEP&gt;Filler loading refers to the amount of filler (e.g., geopolymer or fly ash) added to natural rubber, expressed in parts per hundred rubber (phr), with optimal performance between 10–20 phr.&lt;SEP&gt;Filler loading refers to the amount of geopolymer added to natural rubber, specified in parts per hundred rubber (phr), tested at 0, 10, 20, 30, and 40 phr.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b59a215607da3067fe396c2706185342&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5032c26dab6030ab94a51cdae3644229&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-93bfd54d23eafa9bcd4bd9cf8880c1cc</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208085</data>
</node>
<node id="Metal Oxide Impurities">
  <data key="d0">Metal Oxide Impurities</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Metal oxide impurities are present in geopolymer particles and are responsible for accelerating the curing process in natural rubber composites.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5032c26dab6030ab94a51cdae3644229</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208086</data>
</node>
<node id="Natural Rubber">
  <data key="d0">Natural Rubber</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Natural Rubber (NR), also known as polyisoprene, is a base polymer and serves as the polymer matrix in various composite materials, particularly in studies involving the incorporation of fillers such as geopolymer and fly ash. It is used in the preparation of NR/geopolymer composites and functions as the primary material for evaluating the effects of these fillers on curing behavior and material performance in vulcanizates. The natural rubber used in these studies is processed in the form of air-dried sheets sourced from Nabon Rubber Co., Ltd. As a key component in composite systems, NR acts as the foundational material for experimental investigations into filler reinforcement and thermal stability. It undergoes thermal degradation between 330°C and 450°C, which is a critical factor in assessing its durability and application limits. Overall, Natural Rubber is central to the development and analysis of advanced composite materials, particularly in the context of geopolymer and fly ash integration.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c9f812eeaa3c93f5c4d945e0ae7c91d0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-93bfd54d23eafa9bcd4bd9cf8880c1cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be33bdf2df99a882230f2e5cf5a15dcf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5032c26dab6030ab94a51cdae3644229&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ad60afc1fc1390582346359c09ae89a7</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208086</data>
</node>
<node id="Physical Interactions">
  <data key="d0">Physical Interactions</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Physical interactions between geopolymer particles and natural rubber molecules contribute to improved mechanical properties.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5032c26dab6030ab94a51cdae3644229</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208088</data>
</node>
<node id="Payne Effect">
  <data key="d0">Payne Effect</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The Payne effect describes the nonlinear decrease in storage modulus under increasing strain in filled rubber, related to filler-filler interactions, which was observed in the study of NR composites.&lt;SEP&gt;The Payne effect is observed in geopolymer-filled natural rubber composites, indicating nonlinear viscoelastic behavior typical of filled rubber systems.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5032c26dab6030ab94a51cdae3644229&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-93bfd54d23eafa9bcd4bd9cf8880c1cc</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208088</data>
</node>
<node id="Industrial Applications">
  <data key="d0">Industrial Applications</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Industrial applications are potential uses for natural rubber composites with 10 phr geopolymer, suggested as suitable based on performance.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5032c26dab6030ab94a51cdae3644229</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208089</data>
</node>
<node id="Renewable Resources">
  <data key="d0">Renewable Resources</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Renewable resources are alternative sources for fillers in rubber composites, of interest for improving sustainability and green aspects.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5032c26dab6030ab94a51cdae3644229</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208090</data>
</node>
<node id="Synthetic Fillers">
  <data key="d0">Synthetic Fillers</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Synthetic fillers such as carbon black and silica are conventional reinforcing agents in rubber, contrasted with geopolymer in this study.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5032c26dab6030ab94a51cdae3644229</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208091</data>
</node>
<node id="Carbon Black">
  <data key="d0">Carbon Black</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Carbon black is a conventional synthetic filler used in rubber compounding for reinforcement and property enhancement.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5032c26dab6030ab94a51cdae3644229</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208092</data>
</node>
<node id="Silica">
  <data key="d0">Silica</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Silica is a conventional synthetic filler used in rubber, known for reinforcing properties and hydrophilic surface characteristics.&lt;SEP&gt;Silica is mentioned as an important factor controlling geopolymerization and the structure of the final geopolymer.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5032c26dab6030ab94a51cdae3644229&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5e2d2a2aac672793717ce07dc518653a</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208093</data>
</node>
<node id="Precipitated Silica">
  <data key="d0">Precipitated Silica</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Precipitated silica is a type of synthetic silica with siloxane and acidic silanol groups, widely used in polymer industries.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5032c26dab6030ab94a51cdae3644229</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208093</data>
</node>
<node id="Rice Husk Ash">
  <data key="d0">Rice Husk Ash</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Rice husk ash (RHA) is a filler tested in natural rubber compounds that affects cure characteristics and improves mechanical properties such as Young's modulus and tensile strength.&lt;SEP&gt;Rice husk ash is a natural filler tested in natural rubber compounds as an alternative reinforcing material.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5032c26dab6030ab94a51cdae3644229&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ad60afc1fc1390582346359c09ae89a7</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208095</data>
</node>
<node id="Oil Palm Ash">
  <data key="d0">Oil Palm Ash</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Oil palm ash is a natural filler used in rubber composites, sourced from agricultural waste.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5032c26dab6030ab94a51cdae3644229</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208096</data>
</node>
<node id="Bagasse Fiber Ash">
  <data key="d0">Bagasse Fiber Ash</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Bagasse fiber ash is a natural filler investigated for use in rubber composites.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5032c26dab6030ab94a51cdae3644229</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208097</data>
</node>
<node id="Cellulose Fiber">
  <data key="d0">Cellulose Fiber</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Cellulose fiber is a natural filler used in rubber composites for multiple functional purposes.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5032c26dab6030ab94a51cdae3644229</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208098</data>
</node>
<node id="Coconut Shell Powder">
  <data key="d0">Coconut Shell Powder</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Coconut shell powder is a natural filler explored in rubber composites as a sustainable alternative.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5032c26dab6030ab94a51cdae3644229</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208098</data>
</node>
<node id="Lignin">
  <data key="d0">Lignin</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Lignin is a natural filler used in rubber composites, derived from plant biomass.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5032c26dab6030ab94a51cdae3644229</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208099</data>
</node>
<node id="Geopolymers">
  <data key="d0">Geopolymers</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Geopolymers are inorganic aluminosilicate networks synthesized at room temperature from materials like fly ash and alkaline solutions. They exhibit superior mechanical properties, low density, and cage-like structures, and can be used as green cement or alternative fillers in natural rubber composites to enhance reinforcement.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ad60afc1fc1390582346359c09ae89a7</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208100</data>
</node>
<node id="Ordinary Portland Cement">
  <data key="d0">Ordinary Portland Cement</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Ordinary Portland cement is referenced as a benchmark for durability, with geopolymers being nearly comparable in durability, suggesting potential substitution in construction applications.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ad60afc1fc1390582346359c09ae89a7</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208100</data>
</node>
