<node id="Geopolymer-Filled Natural Rubber Composites">
  <data key="d0">Geopolymer-Filled Natural Rubber Composites</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Geopolymer-filled natural rubber composites are novel materials prepared with varying geopolymer content to study their effects on mechanical, thermal, dynamic, and morphological properties, offering advantages over conventional fillers due to low energy processing.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ad60afc1fc1390582346359c09ae89a7</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208112</data>
</node>
<node id="Zinc Oxide">
  <data key="d0">Zinc Oxide</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Zinc oxide is a commercial-grade material used in the rubber compounding process, likely as a vulcanization activator.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ad60afc1fc1390582346359c09ae89a7</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208114</data>
</node>
<node id="Stearic Acid">
  <data key="d0">Stearic Acid</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Stearic acid is a commercial chemical used in the rubber formulation, typically functioning as a processing aid or activator in vulcanization.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ad60afc1fc1390582346359c09ae89a7</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208114</data>
</node>
<node id="2,2'-Dithiobis-(benzothiazole) (MBTs)">
  <data key="d0">2,2'-Dithiobis-(benzothiazole) (MBTs)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">MBTs is a curing agent used in the rubber compounding process, facilitating the vulcanization of natural rubber.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ad60afc1fc1390582346359c09ae89a7</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208114</data>
</node>
<node id="Sulfur">
  <data key="d0">Sulfur</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Sulfur is a commercial chemical used in rubber formulation, sourced from Ajax Chemical Co., Ltd.&lt;SEP&gt;Sulfur is a commercial chemical used in the vulcanization of rubber composites, contributing to cross-linking of polymer chains.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c9f812eeaa3c93f5c4d945e0ae7c91d0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ad60afc1fc1390582346359c09ae89a7</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208115</data>
</node>
<node id="Alkaline Solution">
  <data key="d0">Alkaline Solution</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Alkaline solution refers to the reagent, such as sodium hydroxide, used to initiate geopolymerization reactions with fly ash to form geopolymers.&lt;SEP&gt;Alkaline solution, such as sodium silicate (Na₂SiO₃) and sodium hydroxide (NaOH), is critical for activating fly ash and facilitating the geopolymerization process.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ad60afc1fc1390582346359c09ae89a7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5e2d2a2aac672793717ce07dc518653a</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208116</data>
</node>
<node id="Geopolymerization">
  <data key="d0">Geopolymerization</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">Geopolymerization is the chemical process in which fly ash reacts with an alkaline solution to form polymerized inorganic aluminosilicate networks, as illustrated in Figure 1.&lt;SEP&gt;Geopolymerization is the chemical process involving the decomposition of aluminosilicate in fly ash and subsequent polycondensation into geopolymer networks under mild temperatures (25–90°C).&lt;SEP&gt;Geopolymerization refers to the chemical reaction forming geopolymers from aluminosilicate sources like fly ash, evidenced by peak shifts in FTIR spectra such as the main band movement at 1,086 cm⁻¹, which allows monitoring of reaction kinetics.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ad60afc1fc1390582346359c09ae89a7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5e2d2a2aac672793717ce07dc518653a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2b41c1604bb36ddf18d8b7e29a9c6dbe</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208118</data>
</node>
<node id="Mae Moh power station">
  <data key="d0">Mae Moh power station</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">Mae Moh power station is a power plant located in Lampang, Thailand, producing fly ash as a by-product.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c9f812eeaa3c93f5c4d945e0ae7c91d0</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208119</data>
</node>
