  <data key="d5">1754208208</data>
</node>
<node id="NR">
  <data key="d0">NR</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">NR refers to natural rubber, a material used in the formation of GP/NR and FA/NR composites.&lt;SEP&gt;NR refers to natural rubber, specifically gum natural rubber used in vulcanizates and composites.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ee381638b4e2088dd8f91df9d74e6da7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b59a215607da3067fe396c2706185342</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208208</data>
</node>
<node id="FA/NR">
  <data key="d0">FA/NR</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">FA/NR refers to a composite material made of natural rubber (NR) filled with fly ash (FA), used in various mechanical and thermal tests.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ee381638b4e2088dd8f91df9d74e6da7</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208208</data>
</node>
<node id="GP/NR">
  <data key="d0">GP/NR</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">GP/NR refers to a composite material made of natural rubber (NR) filled with geopolymer (GP), subjected to vulcanization and multiple characterization tests.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ee381638b4e2088dd8f91df9d74e6da7</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208209</data>
</node>
<node id="Scorch Time (ts2)">
  <data key="d0">Scorch Time (ts2)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Scorch time (ts2) is a parameter measured during curing, indicating the initial stage of vulcanization of rubber compounds.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ee381638b4e2088dd8f91df9d74e6da7</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208210</data>
</node>
<node id="Optimum Cure Time (tc90)">
  <data key="d0">Optimum Cure Time (tc90)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Optimum cure time (tc90) is the time required to achieve 90% of the maximum cure in rubber vulcanization, recorded during MDR testing.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ee381638b4e2088dd8f91df9d74e6da7</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208211</data>
</node>
<node id="Minimum Torque (ML)">
  <data key="d0">Minimum Torque (ML)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Minimum torque (ML) is a rheological property measured during curing, representing the lowest resistance to deformation in uncured rubber.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ee381638b4e2088dd8f91df9d74e6da7</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208212</data>
</node>
<node id="Maximum Torque (MH)">
  <data key="d0">Maximum Torque (MH)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Maximum torque (MH) is a rheological property measured during curing, indicating the highest stiffness achieved after vulcanization.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ee381638b4e2088dd8f91df9d74e6da7</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208212</data>
</node>
<node id="Tensile Strength">
  <data key="d0">Tensile Strength</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Tensile strength is a mechanical property of rubber composites, estimated from stress-strain curves obtained via tensile testing.&lt;SEP&gt;Tensile strength is a mechanical property that measures the maximum stress a material can withstand while being stretched; NR composites with 10 phr GP showed higher tensile strength than NR/FA composites.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ee381638b4e2088dd8f91df9d74e6da7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-93bfd54d23eafa9bcd4bd9cf8880c1cc</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208213</data>
</node>
<node id="Elongation at Break">
  <data key="d0">Elongation at Break</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Elongation at break is a mechanical property indicating the maximum deformation a material can withstand before fracture, derived from tensile tests.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ee381638b4e2088dd8f91df9d74e6da7</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208214</data>
</node>
<node id="Storage Modulus (G')">
  <data key="d0">Storage Modulus (G')</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Storage modulus (G') is a dynamic mechanical property representing the elastic response of materials under oscillatory shear, measured using RPA 2000.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ee381638b4e2088dd8f91df9d74e6da7</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208214</data>
</node>
<node id="Thermogravimetric Analysis (TGA)">
  <data key="d0">Thermogravimetric Analysis (TGA)</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">Thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) is a thermal characterization technique used to assess the thermal stability of GP/NR and FA/NR samples under nitrogen atmosphere.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ee381638b4e2088dd8f91df9d74e6da7</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208216</data>
</node>
<node id="Strain Sweep">
  <data key="d0">Strain Sweep</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">Strain sweep is a dynamic mechanical test performed from 0.56% to 500% strain at 1 Hz frequency and 80°C to evaluate viscoelastic properties.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ee381638b4e2088dd8f91df9d74e6da7</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208217</data>
</node>
<node id="Cryogenic Fracture">
  <data key="d0">Cryogenic Fracture</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">Cryogenic fracture is a sample preparation method used to create fresh cross-sectional surfaces of rubber specimens prior to SEM imaging.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ee381638b4e2088dd8f91df9d74e6da7</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208218</data>
</node>
<node id="Optical Microscopy">
  <data key="d0">Optical Microscopy</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">Optical microscopy is a morphological examination technique used on smoothly cut rubber samples with a metallographic microscope.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ee381638b4e2088dd8f91df9d74e6da7</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208219</data>
</node>
<node id="Die Cutting">
  <data key="d0">Die Cutting</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">Die cutting is a method used to prepare dumbbell-shaped test specimens from vulcanized rubber sheets for mechanical testing.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ee381638b4e2088dd8f91df9d74e6da7</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208221</data>
</node>
<node id="Particle Size Distribution">
  <data key="d0">Particle Size Distribution</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Particle size distribution describes the range and frequency of particle sizes in geopolymer and fly ash fillers, as shown in Figure 2.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ee381638b4e2088dd8f91df9d74e6da7</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208222</data>
</node>
<node id="Chemical Composition">
  <data key="d0">Chemical Composition</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Chemical composition refers to the elemental makeup of fly ash and geopolymer fillers, with differences noted in SiO2 and other oxides.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ee381638b4e2088dd8f91df9d74e6da7</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208222</data>
</node>
<node id="Spherical Particles">
  <data key="d0">Spherical Particles</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Spherical particles describe the shape of geopolymer particles, which have smooth surfaces but irregular overall forms.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ee381638b4e2088dd8f91df9d74e6da7</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208223</data>
</node>
<node id="Ceno-spheres">
  <data key="d0">Ceno-spheres</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Ceno-spheres are hollow spherical structures found in fly ash particles, observed via SEM imaging.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ee381638b4e2088dd8f91df9d74e6da7</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208223</data>
</node>
<node id="Table 4">
  <data key="d0">Table 4</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">Table 4 contains the composition data of the geopolymer, including percentages of SiO₂, CaO, Al₂O₃, and other substances.&lt;SEP&gt;Table 4 summarizes the chemical compositions of fly ash and geopolymer particles, listing concentrations of various oxides such as SiO₂, CaO, Fe₂O₃, Al₂O₃, SO₃, K₂O, MgO, TiO₂, SrO, and P₂O₅.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5e2d2a2aac672793717ce07dc518653a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2b41c1604bb36ddf18d8b7e29a9c6dbe</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208225</data>
</node>
<node id="Table 5">
  <data key="d0">Table 5</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">Table 5 summarizes the peak assignments for the FTIR spectra of geopolymer and fly ash, linking specific wavenumbers to molecular vibrations and bond types.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2b41c1604bb36ddf18d8b7e29a9c6dbe</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208226</data>
</node>
<node id="Silicon Dioxide (SiO₂)">
  <data key="d0">Silicon Dioxide (SiO₂)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Silicon dioxide is a chemical compound present in both fly ash and geopolymer, with concentrations of 24.0% in fly ash and 30.0% in geopolymer as shown in Table 4.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2b41c1604bb36ddf18d8b7e29a9c6dbe</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208226</data>
</node>
<node id="Calcium Oxide (CaO)">
  <data key="d0">Calcium Oxide (CaO)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Calcium oxide is a chemical component found in fly ash at 29.8% concentration and in geopolymer at 17.9%, indicating a reduction during geopolymerization.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2b41c1604bb36ddf18d8b7e29a9c6dbe</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208228</data>
</node>
<node id="Iron Oxide (Fe₂O₃)">
  <data key="d0">Iron Oxide (Fe₂O₃)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Iron oxide is present in fly ash at 19.2% and in geopolymer at 10.1%, showing a decrease in concentration from fly ash to geopolymer.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2b41c1604bb36ddf18d8b7e29a9c6dbe</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208229</data>
</node>
<node id="Alumina (Al₂O₃)">
  <data key="d0">Alumina (Al₂O₃)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Alumina is a constituent of fly ash at 13.7% and geopolymer at 12.4%, indicating slight reduction in concentration during transformation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2b41c1604bb36ddf18d8b7e29a9c6dbe</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208230</data>
</node>
<node id="Sulfur Oxide (SO₃)">
  <data key="d0">Sulfur Oxide (SO₃)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Sulfur oxide is present in fly ash at 7.7% and reduced to 3.5% in geopolymer.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2b41c1604bb36ddf18d8b7e29a9c6dbe</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208231</data>
</node>
<node id="Potassium Oxide (K₂O)">
  <data key="d0">Potassium Oxide (K₂O)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Potassium oxide appears in fly ash at 2.9% and in geopolymer at 1.8%, showing a decrease in concentration.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2b41c1604bb36ddf18d8b7e29a9c6dbe</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208231</data>
</node>
<node id="Magnesium Oxide (MgO)">
  <data key="d0">Magnesium Oxide (MgO)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Magnesium oxide is present in fly ash at 1.8% and in geopolymer at 1.5%.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2b41c1604bb36ddf18d8b7e29a9c6dbe</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208231</data>
</node>
<node id="Titanium Dioxide (TiO₂)">
  <data key="d0">Titanium Dioxide (TiO₂)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Titanium dioxide is found in fly ash at 0.6% and in geopolymer at 0.3%.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2b41c1604bb36ddf18d8b7e29a9c6dbe</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208233</data>
</node>
<node id="Strontium Oxide (SrO)">
  <data key="d0">Strontium Oxide (SrO)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Strontium oxide is present in fly ash at 0.3% and in geopolymer at 0.2%.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2b41c1604bb36ddf18d8b7e29a9c6dbe</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208233</data>
</node>
<node id="Phosphorus Pentoxide (P₂O₅)">
  <data key="d0">Phosphorus Pentoxide (P₂O₅)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Phosphorus pentoxide is not detected in fly ash (marked as -) but appears in geopolymer at 0.3%.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2b41c1604bb36ddf18d8b7e29a9c6dbe</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208235</data>
</node>
<node id="Si-O-Si Stretching Vibrations">
  <data key="d0">Si-O-Si Stretching Vibrations</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Si-O-Si stretching vibrations are molecular vibrations observed in the FTIR spectrum of fly ash at 1,100 cm⁻¹, indicating silicate network structure.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2b41c1604bb36ddf18d8b7e29a9c6dbe</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208236</data>
</node>
<node id="Al₂O₃ Vibrations">
  <data key="d0">Al₂O₃ Vibrations</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Vibrations of aluminum oxide are detected at 681 cm⁻¹ in the FTIR spectrum of fly ash.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2b41c1604bb36ddf18d8b7e29a9c6dbe</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208236</data>
</node>
<node id="Al-O-Al Stretching Vibrations">
  <data key="d0">Al-O-Al Stretching Vibrations</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Al-O-Al stretching vibrations appear at 1,007 cm⁻¹ in the FTIR spectrum of fly ash, indicating aluminum-oxygen bonding.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2b41c1604bb36ddf18d8b7e29a9c6dbe</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208237</data>
</node>
<node id="O-H Groups">
  <data key="d0">O-H Groups</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">O-H groups are present in fly ash particles, indicated by an absorption peak at 3,445 cm⁻¹ in the FTIR spectrum.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2b41c1604bb36ddf18d8b7e29a9c6dbe</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208237</data>
</node>
<node id="Si-O-T Bonds">
  <data key="d0">Si-O-T Bonds</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Si-O-T bonds (where T is tetrahedral Al or Si) are assigned to the asymmetric stretching vibration peak at 1,001 cm⁻¹ in the geopolymer spectrum, indicating geopolymerization.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2b41c1604bb36ddf18d8b7e29a9c6dbe</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208238</data>
</node>
<node id="Silanol Groups">
  <data key="d0">Silanol Groups</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Silanol groups are indicated by two small bands at 1,625 cm⁻¹ and 3,452 cm⁻¹ in the geopolymer spectrum, corresponding to stretching vibrations and hydrogen bonding with water.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2b41c1604bb36ddf18d8b7e29a9c6dbe</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208238</data>
</node>
<node id="Water">
  <data key="d0">Water</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Water is involved in hydrogen bonding with adjacent silanols in geopolymer, as shown by the 3,452 cm⁻¹ peak in the FTIR spectrum.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2b41c1604bb36ddf18d8b7e29a9c6dbe</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208239</data>
</node>
<node id="Sodium Silicate (Na₂SiO₃)">
  <data key="d0">Sodium Silicate (Na₂SiO₃)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Sodium silicate is an alkaline compound used in the activation of fly ash during geopolymerization.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5e2d2a2aac672793717ce07dc518653a</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208240</data>
</node>
<node id="Sodium Hydroxide (NaOH)">
  <data key="d0">Sodium Hydroxide (NaOH)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Sodium hydroxide is an alkaline compound used to facilitate the breakdown of aluminosilicate structures in fly ash during geopolymerization.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5e2d2a2aac672793717ce07dc518653a</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208240</data>
</node>
<node id="Aluminosilicate">
  <data key="d0">Aluminosilicate</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Aluminosilicate is a key component in fly ash that undergoes decomposition and polymerization during geopolymerization to form geopolymer networks.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5e2d2a2aac672793717ce07dc518653a</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208242</data>
</node>
<node id="Scanning Electron Microscopy">
  <data key="d0">Scanning Electron Microscopy</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Scanning electron microscopy is a technique used to obtain high-magnification micrographs of natural rubber composites, as shown in Figure 7.&lt;SEP&gt;Scanning electron microscopy is used to analyze the microstructure of geopolymer and fly ash particles, as shown in Figure 3.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b59a215607da3067fe396c2706185342&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5e2d2a2aac672793717ce07dc518653a</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208242</data>
</node>
<node id="Fourier-transform Infrared Spectroscopy">
  <data key="d0">Fourier-transform Infrared Spectroscopy</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Fourier-transform infrared spectroscopy is used to analyze the chemical composition and bonding in geopolymer and fly ash particles, as shown in Figure 4.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5e2d2a2aac672793717ce07dc518653a</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208244</data>
</node>
<node id="Strontium oxide (SrO)">
  <data key="d0">Strontium oxide (SrO)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Strontium oxide is a chemical compound present in the material composition analysis, with a reported value of 0.3 wt% in one sample and 0.2 wt% in another.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5e2d2a2aac672793717ce07dc518653a</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208245</data>
</node>
<node id="Phosphorus pentoxide (P₂O₅)">
  <data key="d0">Phosphorus pentoxide (P₂O₅)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Phosphorus pentoxide is a chemical compound detected in the material composition, present at 0.3 wt% in one sample.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5e2d2a2aac672793717ce07dc518653a</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208246</data>
</node>
<node id="Manganese oxide (MnO)">
  <data key="d0">Manganese oxide (MnO)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Manganese oxide is a chemical compound noted in the composition analysis, present at 0.1 wt% in one sample.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5e2d2a2aac672793717ce07dc518653a</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208246</data>
</node>
<node id="Barium oxide (BaO)">
  <data key="d0">Barium oxide (BaO)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Barium oxide is a chemical compound identified in the material, present at 0.2 wt% in one sample.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5e2d2a2aac672793717ce07dc518653a</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208247</data>
</node>
<node id="Sodium oxide (Na₂O)">
  <data key="d0">Sodium oxide (Na₂O)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Sodium oxide is a chemical compound found in the material composition at 8.5 wt% in one sample.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5e2d2a2aac672793717ce07dc518653a</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208248</data>
</node>
<node id="Zirconium oxide (ZrO₂)">
  <data key="d0">Zirconium oxide (ZrO₂)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Zirconium oxide is a chemical compound present in the material at 0.1 wt% in one sample.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5e2d2a2aac672793717ce07dc518653a</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208248</data>
</node>
<node id="Alumina">
  <data key="d0">Alumina</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Alumina is identified as a key component, along with silica and aluminosilicate in fly ash, that influences geopolymerization and geopolymer structure.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5e2d2a2aac672793717ce07dc518653a</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208249</data>
</node>
<node id="Nucleation">
  <data key="d0">Nucleation</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">Nucleation is a stage in the geopolymerization process that follows the release of active Al³⁺ and Si⁴⁺ species and precedes oligomerization and polymerization.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5e2d2a2aac672793717ce07dc518653a</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208250</data>
</node>
<node id="Oligomerization">
  <data key="d0">Oligomerization</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">Oligomerization is a phase in geopolymerization where aluminosilicate oligomers form from nuclei of tetrahedral SiO₄ and AlO₄ units.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5e2d2a2aac672793717ce07dc518653a</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208251</data>
</node>
<node id="Polymerization">
  <data key="d0">Polymerization</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">Polymerization is the final stage in geopolymerization where aluminosilicate oligomers link to form polysialate chains and three-dimensional geopolymer networks.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5e2d2a2aac672793717ce07dc518653a</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208252</data>
</node>
<node id="Hydrolysis">
  <data key="d0">Hydrolysis</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">Hydrolysis is the process by which -Si-O-Si- or -Si-O-Al- bonds in aluminosilicate are broken, releasing active Al³⁺ and Si⁴⁺ species.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5e2d2a2aac672793717ce07dc518653a</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208253</data>
</node>
<node id="Polysialate">
  <data key="d0">Polysialate</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Polysialate refers to the chain structures formed during geopolymerization, including -Al-O-Si- chains.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5e2d2a2aac672793717ce07dc518653a</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208253</data>
</node>
<node id="Polysialate siloxo">
  <data key="d0">Polysialate siloxo</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Polysialate siloxo refers to -Al-O-Si-Si- chains formed during the geopolymerization process.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5e2d2a2aac672793717ce07dc518653a</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208255</data>
</node>
<node id="Polysialate disiloxo">
  <data key="d0">Polysialate disiloxo</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Polysialate disiloxo refers to -Al-O-Si-Si-Si- chains formed during geopolymerization.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5e2d2a2aac672793717ce07dc518653a</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208256</data>
</node>
<node id="Geopolymer Paste">
  <data key="d0">Geopolymer Paste</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Geopolymer paste is the mixture formed from fly ash and alkaline solution, cast into a mold at room temperature to set into its final form.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5e2d2a2aac672793717ce07dc518653a</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208257</data>
</node>
<node id="Wiley Online Library">
  <data key="d0">Wiley Online Library</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">Wiley Online Library is mentioned as the website where the color figure can be viewed, indicating it is the publishing platform for the document.&lt;SEP&gt;Wiley Online Library is referenced as the platform where the color version of a figure can be viewed.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b59a215607da3067fe396c2706185342&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5e2d2a2aac672793717ce07dc518653a</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208258</data>
</node>
<node id="Si-O-T bonds">
  <data key="d0">Si-O-T bonds</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Si-O-T bonds refer to asymmetric stretching vibrations in the geopolymer structure, where T is a tetrahedral aluminum or silicon atom.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5e2d2a2aac672793717ce07dc518653a</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208260</data>
</node>
<node id="Vibration of Al₂O₃">
  <data key="d0">Vibration of Al₂O₃</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Vibration of Al₂O₃ refers to the infrared absorption at 681 cm⁻¹ attributed to Al₂O₃ in the material.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5e2d2a2aac672793717ce07dc518653a</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208261</data>
</node>
<node id="Al-O-Al stretching">
  <data key="d0">Al-O-Al stretching</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Al-O-Al stretching refers to the infrared absorption at 1007 cm⁻¹, indicating Al-O-Al bond vibrations in the geopolymer structure.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5e2d2a2aac672793717ce07dc518653a</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208261</data>
</node>
<node id="Asymmetric stretching vibrations">
  <data key="d0">Asymmetric stretching vibrations</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Asymmetric stretching vibrations of Si-O-T bonds occur at 1086 and 1100 cm⁻¹ and are key indicators of geopolymer network formation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5e2d2a2aac672793717ce07dc518653a</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208261</data>
</node>
<node id="Cure Curve">
  <data key="d0">Cure Curve</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">The cure curve represents the vulcanization behavior of rubber compounds, showing changes in torque over time with different filler loadings.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-be33bdf2df99a882230f2e5cf5a15dcf</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208262</data>
</node>
<node id="Cross-Linking">
  <data key="d0">Cross-Linking</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Cross-linking refers to the formation of chemical bonds in the rubber network during vulcanization, influenced by filler type and loading.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-be33bdf2df99a882230f2e5cf5a15dcf</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208263</data>
</node>
<node id="Delta Torque">
  <data key="d0">Delta Torque</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Delta torque is the difference between maximum and minimum torque during vulcanization, indicating the degree of crosslinking in rubber composites.&lt;SEP&gt;Delta torque is the difference between maximum and minimum torque in cure curves, indicating cross-link density and filler-rubber interactions in vulcanizates.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b59a215607da3067fe396c2706185342&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be33bdf2df99a882230f2e5cf5a15dcf</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208263</data>
</node>
<node id="Minimum Torque">
  <data key="d0">Minimum Torque</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Minimum torque is a measure of the viscosity of the uncured rubber compound, influenced by filler loading and distribution.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-be33bdf2df99a882230f2e5cf5a15dcf</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208265</data>
</node>
<node id="Maximum Torque">
  <data key="d0">Maximum Torque</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Maximum torque reflects the extent of cross-linking and filler interactions in the cured rubber vulcanizate.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-be33bdf2df99a882230f2e5cf5a15dcf</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208266</data>
</node>
<node id="Scorch Time">
  <data key="d0">Scorch Time</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Scorch time (ts2) is the initial stage of vulcanization, representing the time before significant curing begins, as listed in Table 6.&lt;SEP&gt;Scorch time is the period before vulcanization begins, indicating processing safety; it decreased with increasing geopolymer loading.&lt;SEP&gt;Scorch time refers to the initial resistance to curing before vulcanization begins, which was shorter in GP-filled NR composites, indicating faster onset of curing.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b59a215607da3067fe396c2706185342&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be33bdf2df99a882230f2e5cf5a15dcf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-93bfd54d23eafa9bcd4bd9cf8880c1cc</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208267</data>
</node>
<node id="Cure Time">
  <data key="d0">Cure Time</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Cure time (tc90) is the duration required to reach 90% of maximum cure in rubber vulcanization, provided in Table 6 for various composites.&lt;SEP&gt;Cure time refers to the duration required for complete vulcanization, which shortened with higher geopolymer content.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b59a215607da3067fe396c2706185342&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be33bdf2df99a882230f2e5cf5a15dcf</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208269</data>
</node>
<node id="Cure Rate Index">
  <data key="d0">Cure Rate Index</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Cure Rate Index is a measure of vulcanization efficiency, which was found to be higher in GP-filled NR composites than in gum NR vulcanizates.&lt;SEP&gt;Cure rate index (CRI) is a measure of how quickly a rubber composite cures, calculated from scorch and cure times, and reported in Table 6.&lt;SEP&gt;Cure rate index measures the speed of vulcanization; it increased with geopolymer loading but was lower for fly ash compared to geopolymer at 10 phr.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b59a215607da3067fe396c2706185342&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be33bdf2df99a882230f2e5cf5a15dcf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-93bfd54d23eafa9bcd4bd9cf8880c1cc</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208272</data>
</node>
<node id="Filler-Polymer Interaction">
  <data key="d0">Filler-Polymer Interaction</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Filler-polymer interaction refers to physical or chemical bonding between geopolymer and natural rubber, reducing chain mobility and increasing torque.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-be33bdf2df99a882230f2e5cf5a15dcf</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208274</data>
</node>
<node id="Filler-Filler Interaction">
  <data key="d0">Filler-Filler Interaction</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Filler-filler interaction involves aggregation between geopolymer particles, affecting dispersion and mechanical properties in the rubber matrix.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-be33bdf2df99a882230f2e5cf5a15dcf</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208276</data>
</node>
<node id="Geopolymer Agglomerate">
  <data key="d0">Geopolymer Agglomerate</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Geopolymer agglomerates are clusters formed at high loadings, leading to poor distribution and hindered cross-linking in the rubber.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-be33bdf2df99a882230f2e5cf5a15dcf</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208278</data>
</node>
<node id="Metal Oxides">
  <data key="d0">Metal Oxides</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Metal oxides such as Al₂O₃, Fe₂O₃, and MgO present in geopolymer act as cure activators, accelerating sulfur vulcanization in natural rubber.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-be33bdf2df99a882230f2e5cf5a15dcf</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208279</data>
</node>
<node id="Sulfur Vulcanization">
  <data key="d0">Sulfur Vulcanization</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Sulfur vulcanization is the chemical process used to cross-link natural rubber, accelerated by metal oxides in geopolymer.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-be33bdf2df99a882230f2e5cf5a15dcf</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208282</data>
</node>
<node id="NR-FA10">
  <data key="d0">NR-FA10</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">NR-FA10 is a natural rubber composite filled with 10 parts per hundred rubber (phr) of fly ash (FA), showing less homogeneous dispersion and weaker filler-matrix interactions.&lt;SEP&gt;NR-FA10 is a natural rubber vulcanizate filled with 10 phr fly ash, showing lower thermal stability in Table 7.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b59a215607da3067fe396c2706185342&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd1afa6cc4e2683ac53784f2ac5a3a0c</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208282</data>
</node>
<node id="NR-GP10">
  <data key="d0">NR-GP10</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">NR-GP10 is a natural rubber composite with 10 phr geopolymer (GP) filler, exhibiting more homogeneous dispersion and better mechanical properties compared to FA-filled composites.&lt;SEP&gt;NR-GP10 is a natural rubber vulcanizate filled with 10 phr geopolymer, included in thermal analysis data in Table 7.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b59a215607da3067fe396c2706185342&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd1afa6cc4e2683ac53784f2ac5a3a0c</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208283</data>
</node>
<node id="NR-GP20">
  <data key="d0">NR-GP20</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">NR-GP20 is a natural rubber composite containing 20 phr geopolymer filler, within the optimal loading range for mechanical performance.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b59a215607da3067fe396c2706185342</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208286</data>
</node>
<node id="NR-GP30">
  <data key="d0">NR-GP30</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">NR-GP30 is a natural rubber composite containing 30 parts per hundred rubber (phr) of geopolymer filler, which exhibited a stress-strain curve similar to the gum vulcanizate but with reduced 100% modulus and lower reinforcing performance, likely due to filler agglomeration.&lt;SEP&gt;NR-GP30 is a natural rubber composite with 30 phr geopolymer filler, showing large agglomerates, cavities, and reduced performance due to excessive loading.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c7f44c7e75058cc491eece367b71f639&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b59a215607da3067fe396c2706185342</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208290</data>
</node>
<node id="NR-GP40">
  <data key="d0">NR-GP40</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">NR-GP40 is a natural rubber composite with 40 phr geopolymer filler, exhibiting significant agglomeration and voids, indicating poor dispersion and adhesion.&lt;SEP&gt;NR-GP40 is a natural rubber composite with 40 phr geopolymer loading, showing decreased mechanical performance compared to lower loadings, attributed to agglomeration and reduced effective surface area of the filler.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c7f44c7e75058cc491eece367b71f639&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b59a215607da3067fe396c2706185342</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208291</data>
</node>
<node id="Gum NR">
  <data key="d0">Gum NR</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Gum NR refers to unfilled natural rubber vulcanizate, characterized by smooth fractured surfaces and small ZnO particles.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b59a215607da3067fe396c2706185342</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208291</data>
</node>
<node id="Figure 8">
  <data key="d0">Figure 8</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Figure 8 schematically illustrates the proposed structures and distribution of geopolymer and fly ash in the natural rubber matrix.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b59a215607da3067fe396c2706185342</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208298</data>
</node>
<node id="Figure 9">
  <data key="d0">Figure 9</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Figure 9 presents data on the mechanical properties of natural rubber composites, showing improvements with geopolymer filler.&lt;SEP&gt;Figure 9 presents stress-strain curves of gum, fly ash-, and geopolymer-filled natural rubber vulcanizates, showing the effect of filler loading on mechanical behavior.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c7f44c7e75058cc491eece367b71f639&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b59a215607da3067fe396c2706185342</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208300</data>
</node>
<node id="Table 6">
  <data key="d0">Table 6</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Table 6 provides cure characteristics of gum and filler-filled natural rubber vulcanizates, including torque, scorch time, cure time, and cure rate index.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b59a215607da3067fe396c2706185342</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208301</data>
</node>
<node id="Fe2O3">
  <data key="d0">Fe2O3</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Fe2O3 (iron oxide) is a component found in higher content in fly ash, contributing to its agglomeration tendency.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b59a215607da3067fe396c2706185342</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208301</data>
</node>
<node id="Al2O3">
  <data key="d0">Al2O3</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Al2O3 (aluminum oxide) is present in higher amounts in fly ash and may contribute to its larger particle size and agglomeration.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b59a215607da3067fe396c2706185342</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208302</data>
</node>
<node id="Light Microscopy">
  <data key="d0">Light Microscopy</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Light microscopy is used to observe the fractured surfaces of gum and filled natural rubber composites, revealing differences in roughness and filler dispersion.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b59a215607da3067fe396c2706185342</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208302</data>
</node>
<node id="Cure Characteristic">
  <data key="d0">Cure Characteristic</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Cure characteristic refers to the vulcanization behavior of rubber composites, including torque, scorch time, cure time, and cure rate index, as detailed in Table 6.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b59a215607da3067fe396c2706185342</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208305</data>
</node>
<node id="Filler-Matrix Interaction">
  <data key="d0">Filler-Matrix Interaction</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Filler-matrix interaction describes the adhesion between filler particles and the rubber matrix, stronger in geopolymer-filled composites than in fly ash-filled ones.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b59a215607da3067fe396c2706185342</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208306</data>
</node>
<node id="Agglomerate">
  <data key="d0">Agglomerate</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Agglomerate refers to clusters of filler particles observed in rubber composites, more prevalent in fly ash and high-loading geopolymer samples.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b59a215607da3067fe396c2706185342</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208308</data>
</node>
<node id="Void">
  <data key="d0">Void</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Void refers to cavities or empty spaces around filler aggregates in rubber composites, indicating poor adhesion, especially in fly ash and high-loading geopolymer composites.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b59a215607da3067fe396c2706185342</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208310</data>
</node>
<node id="Specific Surface">
  <data key="d0">Specific Surface</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Specific surface refers to the surface area of filler particles per unit mass, with geopolymer having a larger specific surface leading to increased filler-rubber interactions.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b59a215607da3067fe396c2706185342</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208313</data>
</node>
<node id="geopolymer">
  <data key="d0">geopolymer</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Geopolymer is an inorganic filler used in NR composites, possessing higher specific surface area and stronger filler-filler interactions than fly ash.&lt;SEP&gt;geopolymer is a material used as a filler in natural rubber composites, affecting mechanical properties such as modulus, tensile strength, and elongation at break depending on loading levels.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c7f44c7e75058cc491eece367b71f639&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd1afa6cc4e2683ac53784f2ac5a3a0c</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208314</data>
</node>
<node id="natural rubber">
  <data key="d0">natural rubber</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">natural rubber is the base polymer matrix in which geopolymer and fly ash are dispersed to form vulcanizates with varying mechanical properties.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c7f44c7e75058cc491eece367b71f639</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208317</data>
</node>
<node id="NR/GP composites">
  <data key="d0">NR/GP composites</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">NR/GP composites are natural rubber materials filled with geopolymer, studied for their stress-strain behavior and mechanical reinforcement characteristics.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c7f44c7e75058cc491eece367b71f639</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208319</data>
</node>
<node id="NR/FA composites">
  <data key="d0">NR/FA composites</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">NR/FA composites are natural rubber materials filled with fly ash, used as a comparison to geopolymer-filled composites in mechanical testing.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c7f44c7e75058cc491eece367b71f639</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208320</data>
</node>
<node id="Figure 10">
  <data key="d0">Figure 10</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">Figure 10 illustrates tensile strength and elongation at break for gum and filled natural rubber vulcanizates, highlighting the impact of geopolymer and fly ash content.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c7f44c7e75058cc491eece367b71f639</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208322</data>
</node>
<node id="Figure 11">
  <data key="d0">Figure 11</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">Figure 11 shows storage modulus as a function of strain amplitude for natural rubber composites tested at 80°C and 1 Hz, indicating dynamic mechanical behavior.&lt;SEP&gt;Figure 11 shows the storage modulus as a function of strain amplitude for gum, FA-, and GP-filled NR vulcanizates tested at 80°C and 1 Hz.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c7f44c7e75058cc491eece367b71f639&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd1afa6cc4e2683ac53784f2ac5a3a0c</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208323</data>
</node>
<node id="Figure 12">
  <data key="d0">Figure 12</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">Figure 12 displays thermogravimetric analysis and DTG thermograms of the gum natural rubber, providing thermal stability data.&lt;SEP&gt;Figure 12 presents TGA and DTG thermograms of gum NR and GP- or FA-filled NR vulcanizates, illustrating their thermal degradation behavior.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c7f44c7e75058cc491eece367b71f639&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd1afa6cc4e2683ac53784f2ac5a3a0c</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208326</data>
</node>
<node id="gum vulcanizate">
  <data key="d0">gum vulcanizate</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">gum vulcanizate refers to unfilled natural rubber used as a baseline control in mechanical testing, against which filled composites are compared for properties like modulus, tensile strength, and elongation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c7f44c7e75058cc491eece367b71f639</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208328</data>
</node>
<node id="Youngs modulus">
  <data key="d0">Youngs modulus</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Young’s modulus is a measure of material stiffness; in the text, it is noted to increase in NR composites with 10 or 20 phr geopolymer, indicating enhanced rigidity.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c7f44c7e75058cc491eece367b71f639</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208329</data>
</node>
<node id="100% modulus">
  <data key="d0">100% modulus</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">100% modulus refers to the stress required to stretch a material to double its original length; it increased at 10 or 20 phr GP loading but decreased at higher loadings due to agglomeration.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c7f44c7e75058cc491eece367b71f639</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208331</data>
</node>
<node id="tensile strength">
  <data key="d0">tensile strength</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">tensile strength is the maximum stress a material can withstand while being stretched before failure; it was highest at 10 phr geopolymer loading and decreased with higher filler content.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c7f44c7e75058cc491eece367b71f639</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208333</data>
</node>
<node id="elongation at break">
  <data key="d0">elongation at break</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">elongation at break measures how much a material can stretch before breaking; it decreased with increasing geopolymer content, especially beyond optimal loading levels.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c7f44c7e75058cc491eece367b71f639</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208333</data>
</node>
<node id="filler agglomeration">
  <data key="d0">filler agglomeration</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">filler agglomeration refers to the clustering of geopolymer or fly ash particles in the rubber matrix at high loadings (30–40 phr), reducing effective surface area and causing mechanical performance degradation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c7f44c7e75058cc491eece367b71f639</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208334</data>
</node>
<node id="delta torque">
  <data key="d0">delta torque</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">delta torque is an indicator of crosslink density during vulcanization; high delta torque was observed for NR composite with 10 phr GP, suggesting good reinforcing effect and network formation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c7f44c7e75058cc491eece367b71f639</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208335</data>
</node>
<node id="failure location">
  <data key="d0">failure location</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">failure location refers to the point where a material breaks during mechanical testing; it varied in composites with different filler loadings, even when stress-strain curves were similar to the gum vulcanizate.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c7f44c7e75058cc491eece367b71f639</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208335</data>
</node>
<node id="rubber matrix">
  <data key="d0">rubber matrix</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">rubber matrix is the continuous phase in composites made of natural rubber, in which geopolymer or fly ash fillers are dispersed, with adhesion affecting mechanical integrity.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c7f44c7e75058cc491eece367b71f639</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208336</data>
</node>
<node id="specific surface">
  <data key="d0">specific surface</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">specific surface refers to the surface area per unit mass of filler; agglomeration reduces effective specific surface, diminishing reinforcing performance in composites.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c7f44c7e75058cc491eece367b71f639</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208337</data>
</node>
<node id="gum NR">
  <data key="d0">gum NR</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">gum NR refers to a natural rubber vulcanizate without filler, used as a baseline sample in thermal and dynamic property analyses.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-bd1afa6cc4e2683ac53784f2ac5a3a0c</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208338</data>
</node>
<node id="GP-filled NR composites">
  <data key="d0">GP-filled NR composites</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">GP-filled NR composites are natural rubber vulcanizates filled with geopolymer at various loadings, showing improved storage modulus and thermal stability compared to unfilled rubber.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-bd1afa6cc4e2683ac53784f2ac5a3a0c</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208338</data>
</node>
<node id="FA-filled NR composites">
  <data key="d0">FA-filled NR composites</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">FA-filled NR composites are natural rubber vulcanizates filled with fly ash (10 phr), exhibiting lower storage modulus and poorer thermal stability than geopolymer-filled composites.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-bd1afa6cc4e2683ac53784f2ac5a3a0c</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208339</data>
</node>
<node id="storage modulus">
  <data key="d0">storage modulus</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Storage modulus (G') measures the elastic response of a material during cyclic deformation and is used to assess filler-filler network strength in polymer composites.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-bd1afa6cc4e2683ac53784f2ac5a3a0c</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208340</data>
</node>
<node id="Payne effect">
  <data key="d0">Payne effect</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The Payne effect refers to the strain-induced breakdown of filler-filler networks in filled polymer composites, observed as nonlinear viscoelastic behavior.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-bd1afa6cc4e2683ac53784f2ac5a3a0c</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208341</data>
</node>
<node id="Thermogravimetric analysis">
  <data key="d0">Thermogravimetric analysis</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) measures mass change as a function of temperature, used here to evaluate thermal stability and decomposition behavior of rubber composites.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-bd1afa6cc4e2683ac53784f2ac5a3a0c</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208342</data>
</node>
<node id="DTG thermograms">
  <data key="d0">DTG thermograms</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">DTG (Derivative Thermogravimetry) thermograms show the rate of mass loss during heating, used to identify degradation temperatures in rubber samples.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-bd1afa6cc4e2683ac53784f2ac5a3a0c</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208343</data>
</node>
<node id="degradation temperature (Td)">
  <data key="d0">degradation temperature (Td)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Degradation temperature (Td) is the temperature at which a material undergoes significant thermal decomposition; values are provided in Table 7 for different samples.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-bd1afa6cc4e2683ac53784f2ac5a3a0c</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208343</data>
</node>
<node id="residual mass at 550°C">
  <data key="d0">residual mass at 550°C</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Residual mass at 550°C indicates the amount of non-volatile material left after thermal decomposition, used to assess inorganic filler content and char formation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-bd1afa6cc4e2683ac53784f2ac5a3a0c</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208344</data>
</node>
<node id="Table 7">
  <data key="d0">Table 7</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Table 7 summarizes decomposition temperatures at different weight loss percentages, residual mass at 550°C, and degradation temperature (Td) for gum, GP-filled, and FA-filled NR vulcanizates.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-bd1afa6cc4e2683ac53784f2ac5a3a0c</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208344</data>
</node>
<node id="specific surface area">
  <data key="d0">specific surface area</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Specific surface area is a property mentioned as influencing filler-filler network formation in NR composites, with geopolymer having higher value than fly ash.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-bd1afa6cc4e2683ac53784f2ac5a3a0c</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208345</data>
</node>
<node id="self-aggregation tendency">
  <data key="d0">self-aggregation tendency</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Self-aggregation tendency is a factor affecting filler network formation in rubber matrices, related to interfacial interactions of fillers.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-bd1afa6cc4e2683ac53784f2ac5a3a0c</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208346</data>
</node>
<node id="filler loading">
  <data key="d0">filler loading</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Filler loading refers to the amount of filler (e.g., geopolymer) added to the NR matrix, which influences storage modulus and network strength.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-bd1afa6cc4e2683ac53784f2ac5a3a0c</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208347</data>
</node>
<node id="metal oxide impurities">
  <data key="d0">metal oxide impurities</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Metal oxide impurities are present in fly ash and may facilitate thermal degradation of polymer networks, contributing to lower thermal stability.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-bd1afa6cc4e2683ac53784f2ac5a3a0c</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208349</data>
</node>
<node id="filler-filler network">
  <data key="d0">filler-filler network</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Filler-filler network describes the interconnected structure formed by inorganic particles in a polymer matrix, affecting dynamic and thermal properties.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-bd1afa6cc4e2683ac53784f2ac5a3a0c</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208349</data>
</node>
<node id="polymer matrix">
  <data key="d0">polymer matrix</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Polymer matrix refers to the natural rubber phase in which inorganic fillers are dispersed, interacting with fillers to influence composite properties.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-bd1afa6cc4e2683ac53784f2ac5a3a0c</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208350</data>
</node>
<node id="Prince of Songkla University, Surat Thani Campus">
  <data key="d0">Prince of Songkla University, Surat Thani Campus</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">Prince of Songkla University, Surat Thani Campus, Thailand, provided access to research facilities for the study.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-93bfd54d23eafa9bcd4bd9cf8880c1cc</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208350</data>
</node>
<node id="Thermal Degradation of NR Composites">
  <data key="d0">Thermal Degradation of NR Composites</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">Thermal degradation of NR composites occurs in two stages: the first between 330°C and 450°C involving polyisoprene breakdown, and the second between 450°C and 600°C involving oxidized structures and sulphidic cross-link depletion.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-93bfd54d23eafa9bcd4bd9cf8880c1cc</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208350</data>
</node>
<node id="Rubber Chemistry">
  <data key="d0">Rubber Chemistry</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Rubber chemistry is the scientific domain relevant to the study, involving polymer networks, vulcanization, and filler-matrix interactions in rubber composites.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-93bfd54d23eafa9bcd4bd9cf8880c1cc</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208351</data>
</node>
<node id="NR/FA Composites">
  <data key="d0">NR/FA Composites</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">NR/FA composites refer to natural rubber composites filled with fly ash, which exhibited poor thermal stability and weaker filler-matrix interactions compared to geopolymer-filled composites.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-93bfd54d23eafa9bcd4bd9cf8880c1cc</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208352</data>
</node>
<node id="GP-Filled NR Composites">
  <data key="d0">GP-Filled NR Composites</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">GP-Filled NR Composites are natural rubber vulcanizates incorporating geopolymer as a filler, showing improved scorch and cure times, higher modulus, and better dispersion at 10 phr loading.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-93bfd54d23eafa9bcd4bd9cf8880c1cc</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208353</data>
</node>
<node id="Modulus">
  <data key="d0">Modulus</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Modulus, specifically 100% modulus, measures the stiffness of rubber materials; NR composites with 10 phr GP exhibited higher 100% modulus compared to those with similar FA loading.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-93bfd54d23eafa9bcd4bd9cf8880c1cc</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208354</data>
</node>
<node id="Storage Modulus G">
  <data key="d0">Storage Modulus G</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Storage Modulus G’ measures the elastic response of a material under deformation; it increased with GP filler loading in the low-strain regime, indicating enhanced stiffness.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-93bfd54d23eafa9bcd4bd9cf8880c1cc</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208354</data>
</node>
<node id="Thermal Degradation Temperature">
  <data key="d0">Thermal Degradation Temperature</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Thermal degradation temperature refers to the temperature at which a material begins to decompose; values at 5%, 10%, 50%, and 80% weight loss were higher for GP- or FA-filled NR composites than gum NR.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-93bfd54d23eafa9bcd4bd9cf8880c1cc</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208356</data>
</node>
<node id="Sulphidic Cross-Links">
  <data key="d0">Sulphidic Cross-Links</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Sulphidic cross-links are chemical bonds formed during vulcanization of rubber that contribute to network stability; their depletion occurs during the second stage of thermal degradation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-93bfd54d23eafa9bcd4bd9cf8880c1cc</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208357</data>
</node>
<node id="Oxidized Structures">
  <data key="d0">Oxidized Structures</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Oxidized structures in natural rubber are formed during thermal aging and contribute to degradation behavior during the second stage of decomposition.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-93bfd54d23eafa9bcd4bd9cf8880c1cc</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208357</data>
</node>
<node id="Agglomerates">
  <data key="d0">Agglomerates</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Agglomerates are clusters of filler particles that form when dispersion is poor; large agglomerates were observed in NR composites with high GP content (30–40 phr).</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-93bfd54d23eafa9bcd4bd9cf8880c1cc</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208359</data>
</node>
<node id="Cavities">
  <data key="d0">Cavities</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Cavities are voids or empty spaces in the composite matrix, observed in high-GP-loaded NR composites due to insufficient adhesion between NR and GP.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-93bfd54d23eafa9bcd4bd9cf8880c1cc</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208359</data>
</node>
<node id="Dispersion">
  <data key="d0">Dispersion</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Dispersion refers to the uniform distribution of filler particles in the rubber matrix; finer and better dispersion was observed in NR composites with 10 phr GP compared to NR/FA composites.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-93bfd54d23eafa9bcd4bd9cf8880c1cc</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208360</data>
</node>
<node id="C. Nakason">
  <data key="d0">C. Nakason</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">C. Nakason is a researcher who has co-authored multiple studies on polymer composites and rubber materials, including a 2016 study in Polym. Compos. and a 2015 study in J. Chem.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9384b64ae5ac9b328c32256e844860ba</data>
  <data key="d4">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1754208360</data>
</node>
