<edge source="Geopolymer" target="Solid to Liquid Ratio">
  <data key="d6">16.0</data>
  <data key="d7">A low solid to liquid ratio at high mullite content may result in insufficient activator, leading to incomplete reaction and unreacted fly ash particles.&lt;SEP&gt;The solid to liquid ratio affects the consistency and reactivity of the geopolymer mixture, decreasing as more mullite is added.</data>
  <data key="d8">formulation balance,mixture design,reaction efficiency,rheological property</data>
  <data key="d9">chunk-ab8d7ecaf50775e77f92ac39831ef997&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0ff7939cdf3c7690a9b0936aae778017</data>
  <data key="d10">doi_10.1002_9780470456200.md</data>
  <data key="d11">1754040281</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Geopolymer" target="Final Setting Time">
  <data key="d6">7.0</data>
  <data key="d7">The final setting time is a key physical property of geopolymer, measured at 3.2 hours when 20 wt% mullite is added.</data>
  <data key="d8">hardening process,property measurement</data>
  <data key="d9">chunk-ab8d7ecaf50775e77f92ac39831ef997</data>
  <data key="d10">doi_10.1002_9780470456200.md</data>
  <data key="d11">1754040283</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Geopolymer" target="NaOH">
  <data key="d6">9.0</data>
  <data key="d7">NaOH is used as part of the alkaline activator in the geopolymer mixture, necessary for initiating the geopolymerization reaction.</data>
  <data key="d8">chemical activation,reaction initiation</data>
  <data key="d9">chunk-ab8d7ecaf50775e77f92ac39831ef997</data>
  <data key="d10">doi_10.1002_9780470456200.md</data>
  <data key="d11">1754040284</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Geopolymer" target="Natural Rubber">
  <data key="d6">38.0</data>
  <data key="d7">Geopolymer is used as a filler in natural rubber (NR) composites, where it is incorporated into the rubber matrix to form NR/geopolymer composites with varying loadings. This integration influences the cure characteristics, cross-link density, and overall mechanical and thermal properties of the resulting material. Geopolymer particles exhibit stronger interactions and better dispersion within the natural rubber matrix compared to traditional fillers such as fly ash, which contributes to enhanced mechanical strength and thermal stability. Overall, the use of geopolymer as a filler in natural rubber composites leads to improved performance, making it a promising alternative in the development of advanced rubber materials.</data>
  <data key="d8">composite enhancement,composite formation,filler-matrix interaction,material blending,material integration,material interaction</data>
  <data key="d9">chunk-c9f812eeaa3c93f5c4d945e0ae7c91d0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5032c26dab6030ab94a51cdae3644229&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-93bfd54d23eafa9bcd4bd9cf8880c1cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be33bdf2df99a882230f2e5cf5a15dcf</data>
  <data key="d10">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d11">1754208477</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Geopolymer" target="Natural Rubber Composites with Geopolymer Filler">
  <data key="d6">10.0</data>
  <data key="d7">The study investigates the use of geopolymer as a novel filler in natural rubber composites.</data>
  <data key="d8">material application,research focus</data>
  <data key="d9">chunk-5032c26dab6030ab94a51cdae3644229</data>
  <data key="d10">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d11">1754208484</data>
</edge>
