  <data key="d6">7.0</data>
  <data key="d7">Gum NR is used as a reference to evaluate the impact of fly ash filler on dynamic and thermal properties.</data>
  <data key="d8">material comparison,performance evaluation</data>
  <data key="d9">chunk-bd1afa6cc4e2683ac53784f2ac5a3a0c</data>
  <data key="d10">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d11">1754208822</data>
</edge>
<edge source="gum NR" target="polymer matrix">
  <data key="d6">7.0</data>
  <data key="d7">The polymer matrix in gum NR is free of fillers, serving as the base material for comparison with filled composites.</data>
  <data key="d8">base material,unfilled rubber</data>
  <data key="d9">chunk-bd1afa6cc4e2683ac53784f2ac5a3a0c</data>
  <data key="d10">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d11">1754208838</data>
</edge>
<edge source="GP-filled NR composites" target="filler loading">
  <data key="d6">9.0</data>
  <data key="d7">Increasing geopolymer filler loading enhances the storage modulus in the low-strain regime, indicating stronger network formation.</data>
  <data key="d8">loading effect,mechanical enhancement</data>
  <data key="d9">chunk-bd1afa6cc4e2683ac53784f2ac5a3a0c</data>
  <data key="d10">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d11">1754208833</data>
</edge>
<edge source="FA-filled NR composites" target="metal oxide impurities">
  <data key="d6">8.0</data>
  <data key="d7">Metal oxide impurities in fly ash may catalyze thermal degradation, explaining the poorer thermal stability of FA-filled NR composites.</data>
  <data key="d8">degradation catalyst,material instability</data>
  <data key="d9">chunk-bd1afa6cc4e2683ac53784f2ac5a3a0c</data>
  <data key="d10">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d11">1754208833</data>
</edge>
<edge source="storage modulus" target="Payne effect">
  <data key="d6">10.0</data>
  <data key="d7">The nonlinear viscoelastic behavior in filled composites, reflected in storage modulus reduction with strain, is attributed to the Payne effect.</data>
  <data key="d8">dynamic mechanical behavior,network breakdown</data>
  <data key="d9">chunk-bd1afa6cc4e2683ac53784f2ac5a3a0c</data>
  <data key="d10">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d11">1754208803</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Thermogravimetric analysis" target="DTG thermograms">
  <data key="d6">9.0</data>
  <data key="d7">TGA and DTG are complementary techniques used together to analyze thermal degradation; TGA measures mass loss, DTG measures its rate.</data>
  <data key="d8">decomposition kinetics,thermal analysis</data>
  <data key="d9">chunk-bd1afa6cc4e2683ac53784f2ac5a3a0c</data>
  <data key="d10">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d11">1754208806</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Thermogravimetric analysis" target="degradation temperature (Td)">
  <data key="d6">9.0</data>
  <data key="d7">Degradation temperature (Td) is derived from TGA data as a key indicator of thermal stability in rubber samples.</data>
  <data key="d8">material characterization,thermal stability</data>
  <data key="d9">chunk-bd1afa6cc4e2683ac53784f2ac5a3a0c</data>
  <data key="d10">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d11">1754208817</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Thermogravimetric analysis" target="residual mass at 550°C">
  <data key="d6">8.0</data>
  <data key="d7">Residual mass at 550°C is determined from TGA data and reflects the inorganic content remaining after heating.</data>
  <data key="d8">compositional analysis,inorganic residue</data>
  <data key="d9">chunk-bd1afa6cc4e2683ac53784f2ac5a3a0c</data>
  <data key="d10">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d11">1754208828</data>
</edge>
<edge source="degradation temperature (Td)" target="Table 7">
  <data key="d6">9.0</data>
  <data key="d7">Table 7 provides numerical values of degradation temperature (Td) for gum, GP-filled, and FA-filled NR vulcanizates.</data>
  <data key="d8">data tabulation,thermal stability metric</data>
  <data key="d9">chunk-bd1afa6cc4e2683ac53784f2ac5a3a0c</data>
  <data key="d10">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d11">1754208828</data>
</edge>
<edge source="residual mass at 550°C" target="Table 7">
  <data key="d6">8.0</data>
  <data key="d7">Table 7 lists the residual mass at 550°C for each sample, indicating the amount of inorganic residue after thermal decomposition.</data>
  <data key="d8">char yield,inorganic content</data>
  <data key="d9">chunk-bd1afa6cc4e2683ac53784f2ac5a3a0c</data>
  <data key="d10">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d11">1754208841</data>
</edge>
<edge source="specific surface area" target="filler-filler network">
  <data key="d6">9.0</data>
  <data key="d7">Higher specific surface area of geopolymer leads to stronger filler-filler interactions and enhanced network formation in the rubber matrix.</data>
  <data key="d8">network strength,surface properties</data>
  <data key="d9">chunk-bd1afa6cc4e2683ac53784f2ac5a3a0c</data>
  <data key="d10">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d11">1754208829</data>
</edge>
<edge source="self-aggregation tendency" target="filler-filler network">
  <data key="d6">8.0</data>
  <data key="d7">The self-aggregation tendency of filler particles influences the extent and strength of filler-filler network development in composites.</data>
  <data key="d8">network formation,particle interaction</data>
  <data key="d9">chunk-bd1afa6cc4e2683ac53784f2ac5a3a0c</data>
  <data key="d10">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d11">1754208833</data>
</edge>
<edge source="filler-filler network" target="polymer matrix">
  <data key="d6">8.0</data>
  <data key="d7">Filler-filler networks interact with the polymer matrix, and their breakdown under strain affects the dynamic mechanical behavior of the composite.</data>
  <data key="d8">composite mechanics,interfacial interaction</data>
  <data key="d9">chunk-bd1afa6cc4e2683ac53784f2ac5a3a0c</data>
  <data key="d10">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d11">1754208835</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Thermal Degradation of NR Composites" target="Sulphidic Cross-Links">
  <data key="d6">9.0</data>
  <data key="d7">The second stage of thermal degradation (450–600°C) involves the depletion of sulphidic cross-links in the natural rubber network.</data>
  <data key="d8">network degradation,vulcanization stability</data>
  <data key="d9">chunk-93bfd54d23eafa9bcd4bd9cf8880c1cc</data>
  <data key="d10">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d11">1754208849</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Thermal Degradation of NR Composites" target="Oxidized Structures">
  <data key="d6">8.0</data>
  <data key="d7">Oxidized structures in natural rubber contribute to degradation during the second stage (450–600°C) alongside sulphidic cross-link breakdown.</data>
  <data key="d8">oxidative degradation,polymer aging</data>
  <data key="d9">chunk-93bfd54d23eafa9bcd4bd9cf8880c1cc</data>
  <data key="d10">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d11">1754208855</data>
</edge>
<edge source="NR/FA Composites" target="Dispersion">
  <data key="d6">8.0</data>
  <data key="d7">NR/FA composites showed poorer dispersion compared to GP-filled composites, leading to inferior mechanical and thermal performance.</data>
  <data key="d8">composite quality,filler distribution</data>
  <data key="d9">chunk-93bfd54d23eafa9bcd4bd9cf8880c1cc</data>
  <data key="d10">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d11">1754208848</data>
</edge>
<edge source="GP-Filled NR Composites" target="Modulus">
  <data key="d6">8.0</data>
  <data key="d7">The 100% modulus was higher in GP-filled NR composites at 10 phr, indicating increased stiffness.</data>
  <data key="d8">material stiffness,mechanical properties</data>
  <data key="d9">chunk-93bfd54d23eafa9bcd4bd9cf8880c1cc</data>
  <data key="d10">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d11">1754208843</data>
</edge>
<edge source="GP-Filled NR Composites" target="Dispersion">
  <data key="d6">9.0</data>
  <data key="d7">Better dispersion of GP particles was observed in NR composites at 10 phr, contributing to improved mechanical properties.</data>
  <data key="d8">homogeneity,performance enhancement</data>
  <data key="d9">chunk-93bfd54d23eafa9bcd4bd9cf8880c1cc</data>
  <data key="d10">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d11">1754208846</data>
</edge>
<edge source="GP-Filled NR Composites" target="Storage Modulus G">
  <data key="d6">9.0</data>
  <data key="d7">In low-strain conditions, the storage modulus G’ increased with GP loading, reflecting enhanced stiffness due to filler-matrix interactions.</data>
  <data key="d8">elastic response,reinforcement</data>
  <data key="d9">chunk-93bfd54d23eafa9bcd4bd9cf8880c1cc</data>
  <data key="d10">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d11">1754208848</data>
</edge>
<edge source="GP-Filled NR Composites" target="Thermal Degradation Temperature">
  <data key="d6">8.0</data>
  <data key="d7">Decomposition temperatures at 5%, 10%, 50%, and 80% weight loss were higher in GP-filled composites than in gum NR, indicating improved thermal stability.</data>
  <data key="d8">decomposition behavior,thermal resistance</data>
  <data key="d9">chunk-93bfd54d23eafa9bcd4bd9cf8880c1cc</data>
  <data key="d10">doi_10.1002_adv.21940.md</data>
  <data key="d11">1754208852</data>
</edge>
